body:
Not sure if the list of content attributes at https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-body-element is meant to be exhaustive but, from the below example and the below code snippet, it does indeed seem that `onerror` is a `Window-reflecting body element event handler` but, it is not part of the list of content attributes at https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-body-element

It is however mentioned at https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#handler-onerror along with a few additional content attribute `Window-reflecting body element event handler`

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>onerror handler on body element</title>
    <script>
        /** 
        * Logs a message to the console indicating where
        * the function was called from.
        * @param {String} calledBy - The callers name
        */
        function fired(calledBy) {
            console.log(`fired called by ${calledBy}`);
        }
    </script>
</head>

<body onerror="fired('error:: body element')">
    <h1>Event bubbling and capture on the <code>body</code> element</h1>
    <a href="#clicked" id="error">Do NOT Click Me!</a>

    <script>
        (function () {
            "use strict";

            const error = document.getElementById('error');

            error.addEventListener('click', () => {
                fired('anchor element');
                throw Error('You should not have clicked me!!');
            });

            window.addEventListener('error', () => {
                fired('error:: window event listener');
            });
        })();
    </script>

</body>

</html>

Output
=====>
fired called by anchor element
fired called by error:: body element
fired called by error:: window event listener
```

https://html.spec.whatwg.org/commit-snapshots/31b264a56253e7b047d1cc6a02a4c6337dd6cff5/#the-body-element

> Thus, for example, a bubbling error event dispatched on a child of the body element of a Document would first trigger the onerror event handler content attributes of that element, then that of the root html element, and only then would it trigger the onerror event handler content attribute on the body element\. This is because the event would bubble from the target, to the body, to the html, to the Document, to the Window, and the event handler on the body is watching the Window not the body\. A regular event listener attached to the body using addEventListener\(\), however, would be run when the event bubbled through the body and not when it reaches the Window object\.
